### Structs as Building Blocks for Real-World Entities

A `struct` groups heterogeneous data types under a single named type. When managing collections of entities (e.g. 100 students), an **array of structures** provides sequential memory layout:

```c
struct Student {
    int id;
    char name[50];
    float gpa;
};

struct Student classroom[100]; // 100 contiguous student records
classroom[0].id = 101;
```

### The Arrow Operator (`->`):
When accessing a struct member through a **pointer to a struct**, `ptr->member` is the idiomatic shorthand for `(*ptr).member`:

```c
struct Student *sPtr = &classroom[0];
sPtr->gpa = 3.95; // Exactly equivalent to (*sPtr).gpa = 3.95;
```

### Nested Structures:
Structures can contain other structures as members (e.g. `struct Date birthDate;` inside `struct Employee`), accessed via chained dot operators (`emp.birthDate.year`).

